\title{A scalable vector cannot be carried as loop state}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-13}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{language}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{simd}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-13}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{A \code{\verb~|while~} expression with several state variables, \code{\verb~|while [i = 0, acc = v] (...)~}, has a tuple type, and the code generator passes the state through \code{\verb~|scf.while~} as one \code{\verb~|!reussir.record~} value. When \code{\verb~|acc~} is a scalable vector the record mixes a fixed member with a scalable one. LLVM has no such struct, and the layout computation of the record aborted the compiler. Found in \pr{996}.}
\p{\strong{Current rule.} A scalable vector is not allowed inside a tuple, so the loop above is rejected with the storage diagnostic. A fixed vector as loop state works. This blocks the accumulator of [the dot product example](ex-QU06) and any other reduction that keeps a scalable vector across iterations.}
\p{\strong{Directions.}}
\ul{
\li{Lower the state of a \code{\verb~|while~} as separate operands of \code{\verb~|scf.while~}, one per state variable, and rebuild the tuple only where the program uses it as a tuple. A pattern that binds every component, which is the common spelling, would then never build the record.}
\li{Allow a tuple whose every use is a projection to stay unmaterialized in general. This is the same idea for all tuples and is a larger change to the code generator.}
\li{Until then, reduce inside each iteration and carry the scalar: \code{\verb~|total + simd::reduce_add(chunk)~}. It is correct and slower than one reduction at the end.}
}
\p{\strong{What would close this.} The first direction, with a test that carries a scalable accumulator through a loop on SVE and RVV.}
